WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.405
Lesson 5.0.

00:00:02.405 --> 00:00:06.130
The Mastery Starter for Module 5: Infrastructure Risk.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:07.934
By the end, you'll know the five

00:00:07.934 --> 00:00:10.066
words this module runs on, and exactly

00:00:10.066 --> 00:00:12.034
how to tell when you've mastered it.

00:00:13.334 --> 00:00:14.624
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.624 --> 00:00:17.743
First, the sixty-second version: why your position

00:00:17.743 --> 00:00:19.456
is only ever as safe as the

00:00:19.456 --> 00:00:22.019
weakest thing it actually depends on.

00:00:22.019 --> 00:00:23.696
Second, the five words you'll

00:00:23.696 --> 00:00:25.394
need, each one drawn out.

00:00:25.394 --> 00:00:27.809
Third, what to have ready before you start.

00:00:27.809 --> 00:00:30.985
Fourth, your first safe step: checking one protocol

00:00:30.985 --> 00:00:34.094
you already use, directly on a block explorer.

00:00:34.094 --> 00:00:36.609
And finally, the mastery ladder, so you know

00:00:36.609 --> 00:00:39.179
exactly what finishing this module looks like.

00:00:40.479 --> 00:00:43.044
Here's the whole module in one sentence.

00:00:43.044 --> 00:00:45.619
Your position is only ever as safe as the

00:00:45.619 --> 00:00:49.053
single weakest thing it actually depends on: the bridge

00:00:49.053 --> 00:00:52.364
that moved your tokens across, the oracle that prices

00:00:52.364 --> 00:00:55.491
your collateral, or the admin key that can upgrade

00:00:55.491 --> 00:00:58.979
the contract holding your funds, out from under you.

00:00:58.979 --> 00:01:01.405
Most of DeFi's largest losses in its

00:01:01.405 --> 00:01:04.553
entire history came from exactly this plumbing,

00:01:04.553 --> 00:01:06.389
not from price moves at all.

00:01:07.689 --> 00:01:10.142
Here's what this module actually covers,

00:01:10.142 --> 00:01:11.519
laid out as a map.

00:01:11.519 --> 00:01:15.087
Every position you hold genuinely depends on a specific

00:01:15.087 --> 00:01:18.463
chain, a specific bridge if your assets ever crossed

00:01:18.463 --> 00:01:22.540
one, a specific oracle pricing whatever it's collateralised by,

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:26.172
and a specific contract, which itself depends on whoever

00:01:26.172 --> 00:01:28.319
holds the keys to upgrade it.

00:01:28.319 --> 00:01:30.314
None of that plumbing is optional.

00:01:30.314 --> 00:01:32.491
All of it is exactly what this module

00:01:32.491 --> 00:01:34.324
teaches you to see, and to rate.

00:01:35.624 --> 00:01:38.127
Worth sitting with why this module exists

00:01:38.127 --> 00:01:40.570
at all, given how much attention usually

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:42.479
goes to price risk instead.

00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:46.218
A protocol can have genuinely sound tokenomics, a fairly

00:01:46.218 --> 00:01:49.761
priced asset, and a sensible strategy, and still lose

00:01:49.761 --> 00:01:54.615
everything, instantly, through a single compromised bridge, a manipulated

00:01:54.615 --> 00:01:57.539
oracle, or a malicious contract upgrade.

00:01:57.539 --> 00:02:00.104
Price risk is visible on every chart.

00:02:00.104 --> 00:02:02.572
Infrastructure risk is invisible, right up

00:02:02.572 --> 00:02:04.064
until the moment it isn't.

00:02:05.364 --> 00:02:07.139
Now, the five words you'll need.

00:02:08.439 --> 00:02:10.044
Word one: bridge.

00:02:10.044 --> 00:02:11.786
Here's the actual sequence a

00:02:11.786 --> 00:02:13.949
bridge runs, every single time.

00:02:13.949 --> 00:02:16.964
You send an asset in, on its original chain.

00:02:16.964 --> 00:02:20.029
The bridge locks, or burns, that original asset.

00:02:20.029 --> 00:02:22.814
And it mints, or releases, a representation

00:02:22.814 --> 00:02:25.139
of it, on the destination chain.

00:02:25.139 --> 00:02:28.111
Every one of those steps depends on the bridge's

00:02:28.111 --> 00:02:31.811
own security model actually holding, since your asset's real

00:02:31.811 --> 00:02:35.026
value now lives entirely in that bridge's own hands,

00:02:35.026 --> 00:02:36.724
for as long as it's crossed.

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:39.639
Word two: layer two.

00:02:39.639 --> 00:02:43.168
A cheaper network, built directly on top of Ethereum,

00:02:43.168 --> 00:02:47.612
specifically to process transactions faster and more cheaply, while

00:02:47.612 --> 00:02:51.899
still relying on Ethereum itself for its underlying security.

00:02:51.899 --> 00:02:56.139
Different layer twos make genuinely different trade-offs in exactly

00:02:56.139 --> 00:02:59.569
how they inherit that security, which is precisely why

00:02:59.569 --> 00:03:02.687
this module treats each one as its own dependency

00:03:02.687 --> 00:03:06.054
to check, not as interchangeable copies of each other.

00:03:07.354 --> 00:03:09.049
Word three: oracle.

00:03:09.049 --> 00:03:12.158
Here's exactly what an oracle does, and why contracts

00:03:12.158 --> 00:03:14.704
can't simply know a price on their own.

00:03:14.704 --> 00:03:16.447
A smart contract has no way

00:03:16.447 --> 00:03:18.864
to see the outside world directly.

00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:21.600
An oracle feeds it real, external prices,

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.793
specifically so it can value collateral, trigger

00:03:24.793 --> 00:03:27.829
liquidations, or settle a trade correctly.

00:03:27.829 --> 00:03:30.918
If that oracle is wrong, manipulated, or simply

00:03:30.918 --> 00:03:34.844
delayed, every single decision the contract makes downstream

00:03:34.844 --> 00:03:36.839
of it inherits that same error.

00:03:38.139 --> 00:03:39.229
Quick check.

00:03:39.229 --> 00:03:41.246
Why does a smart contract actually

00:03:41.246 --> 00:03:42.514
need an oracle at all?

00:03:46.934 --> 00:03:49.402
The answer: a contract has no way to

00:03:49.402 --> 00:03:52.336
see real-world prices on its own; an oracle

00:03:52.336 --> 00:03:54.804
feeds it that external data directly.

00:03:56.104 --> 00:03:57.729
Word four: proxy.

00:03:57.729 --> 00:04:00.970
A contract whose actual logic can be upgraded later,

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:04.877
after it's already deployed and already holding user funds.

00:04:04.877 --> 00:04:07.449
That's genuinely useful, since it lets a

00:04:07.449 --> 00:04:10.210
team fix real bugs without needing everyone

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:12.957
to migrate to an entirely new contract.

00:04:12.957 --> 00:04:15.528
It also means whoever holds the upgrade

00:04:15.528 --> 00:04:18.099
key can, in principle, change what that

00:04:18.099 --> 00:04:20.413
contract does at any time, which is

00:04:20.413 --> 00:04:22.856
exactly why word five matters so much.

00:04:24.156 --> 00:04:25.991
Word five: timelock.

00:04:25.991 --> 00:04:29.122
A mandatory delay, built directly into the contract

00:04:29.122 --> 00:04:32.012
itself, between when an upgrade is proposed and

00:04:32.012 --> 00:04:34.841
when it's actually allowed to take effect.

00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:37.920
That delay is what gives you, and everyone else

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:41.383
watching, a real, actual window to notice a malicious

00:04:41.383 --> 00:04:44.826
or mistaken change, before it can ever take hold.

00:04:44.826 --> 00:04:47.116
A proxy without a timelock can change

00:04:47.116 --> 00:04:49.286
instantly, with zero warning at all.

00:04:50.586 --> 00:04:52.811
All five words, one real position.

00:04:54.111 --> 00:04:56.848
Here's all five words, tied together into

00:04:56.848 --> 00:04:59.650
one single, realistic position, so you can

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:02.296
see exactly how they actually stack.

00:05:02.296 --> 00:05:03.924
You bridge an asset onto a

00:05:03.924 --> 00:05:05.791
layer two, to save on gas.

00:05:05.791 --> 00:05:07.627
You deposit it as collateral

00:05:07.627 --> 00:05:09.826
into a lending protocol there.

00:05:09.826 --> 00:05:13.086
An oracle prices that collateral, continuously.

00:05:13.086 --> 00:05:15.529
And the lending protocol itself is a

00:05:15.529 --> 00:05:18.499
proxy contract, upgradeable by its own team,

00:05:18.499 --> 00:05:21.176
hopefully behind a genuine timelock.

00:05:21.176 --> 00:05:23.826
Every one of those links is a separate thing

00:05:23.826 --> 00:05:26.416
that has to hold, for your position to stay

00:05:26.416 --> 00:05:28.536
exactly as safe as you assume it is.

00:05:29.836 --> 00:05:30.926
Quick check.

00:05:30.926 --> 00:05:33.631
What does a timelock actually give you, specifically?

00:05:38.051 --> 00:05:40.459
The answer: a real window of time to

00:05:40.459 --> 00:05:44.233
notice a malicious, or simply mistaken, contract upgrade,

00:05:44.233 --> 00:05:47.096
before it's actually allowed to take effect.

00:05:48.396 --> 00:05:49.441
Before you start.

00:05:50.741 --> 00:05:52.760
Here's what this module assumes you're

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:55.286
already comfortable with, before diving in.

00:05:55.286 --> 00:05:59.126
Everything from Module zero: wallets, networks, and gas.

00:05:59.126 --> 00:06:01.501
Module one's due-diligence habits.

00:06:01.501 --> 00:06:05.166
Module two's read on A.M.M.s and liquidity.

00:06:05.166 --> 00:06:08.361
Module three's lending and leverage concepts.

00:06:08.361 --> 00:06:11.169
And Module four's read on where yield genuinely

00:06:11.169 --> 00:06:13.626
comes from, and what risk it's paying for.

00:06:14.926 --> 00:06:16.201
Your first safe step.

00:06:17.501 --> 00:06:20.192
Here's exactly what to do, before this module

00:06:20.192 --> 00:06:23.476
goes any deeper into specific infrastructure risks.

00:06:23.476 --> 00:06:26.470
Pick any protocol you already actually use.

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:30.105
Open its main contract directly on a block explorer.

00:06:30.105 --> 00:06:32.625
Check whether it's a proxy contract at all.

00:06:32.625 --> 00:06:33.719
If it is, check who's

00:06:33.719 --> 00:06:35.560
actually allowed to upgrade it.

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:38.200
And check whether a timelock is genuinely in

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:40.782
place, and how long its delay actually runs.

00:06:42.082 --> 00:06:44.948
Worth being precise about what to actually do

00:06:44.948 --> 00:06:47.128
if you find no timelock at all, or

00:06:47.128 --> 00:06:50.056
one with a genuinely short delay, since that's

00:06:50.056 --> 00:06:52.972
a real, specific finding, not just trivia.

00:06:52.972 --> 00:06:55.923
It doesn't automatically mean walk away; plenty

00:06:55.923 --> 00:06:58.628
of legitimate protocols are still early, or

00:06:58.628 --> 00:07:02.432
deliberately choose speed over that particular safeguard.

00:07:02.432 --> 00:07:04.844
It does mean you've just identified one

00:07:04.844 --> 00:07:08.039
real, specific risk, worth weighing directly against

00:07:08.039 --> 00:07:10.812
whatever that position is actually paying you.

00:07:12.112 --> 00:07:14.848
Worth doing this on a protocol you already

00:07:14.848 --> 00:07:18.221
genuinely hold something in, not a hypothetical one,

00:07:18.221 --> 00:07:21.085
since the entire skill this module builds is

00:07:21.085 --> 00:07:24.567
reading real infrastructure under real conditions.

00:07:24.567 --> 00:07:27.143
You may be surprised by what you find, in

00:07:27.143 --> 00:07:30.885
either direction: a well-protected upgrade path, or one with

00:07:30.885 --> 00:07:33.522
far less warning built in than you assumed.

00:07:34.822 --> 00:07:35.917
The mastery ladder.

00:07:37.217 --> 00:07:38.922
Here's the first two rungs on

00:07:38.922 --> 00:07:41.212
this module's ladder, side by side.

00:07:41.212 --> 00:07:43.894
At the beginner level, you simply know which

00:07:43.894 --> 00:07:46.636
chain, which bridge, and which oracle each of

00:07:46.636 --> 00:07:49.797
your own positions actually uses, the basic map.

00:07:49.797 --> 00:07:52.303
At the practitioner level, you go further:

00:07:52.303 --> 00:07:54.750
drawing the complete dependency map for a

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:57.780
position, and actually reading its audits properly,

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:00.227
rather than just checking that one exists.

00:08:01.527 --> 00:08:04.210
Here's what drawing a full dependency map, at

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:08.242
practitioner level, actually looks like in practice, concretely.

00:08:08.242 --> 00:08:09.853
Start with the chain itself,

00:08:09.853 --> 00:08:11.652
and its own security model.

00:08:11.652 --> 00:08:13.573
Add the bridge, if the asset ever

00:08:13.573 --> 00:08:16.302
crossed one, and its own trust assumptions.

00:08:16.302 --> 00:08:19.717
Add the oracle pricing anything held as collateral.

00:08:19.717 --> 00:08:23.329
And add the contract itself, noting specifically whether it's

00:08:23.329 --> 00:08:26.417
a proxy, and whether a timelock actually protects it.

00:08:27.717 --> 00:08:30.034
And here's the top rung, master level,

00:08:30.034 --> 00:08:33.007
the one this module ultimately builds toward.

00:08:33.007 --> 00:08:36.407
At that level, you can operate confidently across

00:08:36.407 --> 00:08:39.943
both E.V.M. and non-E.V.M. chains, with your bridge

00:08:39.943 --> 00:08:44.090
and wrapper exposure deliberately capped, on purpose, rather

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:47.558
than simply accumulated as a byproduct of wherever

00:08:47.558 --> 00:08:49.802
opportunity happened to lead you.

00:08:51.102 --> 00:08:53.788
Here's how you'll actually know you've mastered

00:08:53.788 --> 00:08:56.102
this entire module, in one sentence.

00:08:56.102 --> 00:08:58.962
You can draw the complete dependency map of any

00:08:58.962 --> 00:09:02.240
position, for any protocol, and rate every single link

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:05.100
in that chain, without needing to be told which

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:08.437
parts matter, because you already recognise the pattern.

00:09:09.737 --> 00:09:10.982
Let's recap.

00:09:10.982 --> 00:09:13.428
Your position is only as safe as its

00:09:13.428 --> 00:09:16.997
weakest dependency, and most of DeFi's largest losses

00:09:16.997 --> 00:09:20.536
came from exactly that plumbing, not price moves.

00:09:20.536 --> 00:09:22.472
The five words: bridge, layer

00:09:22.472 --> 00:09:24.901
two, oracle, proxy, and timelock.

00:09:24.901 --> 00:09:27.601
Your first safe step is checking one real

00:09:27.601 --> 00:09:31.436
protocol's own contract, directly, on a block explorer.

00:09:31.436 --> 00:09:34.253
And you'll know you've mastered this module when

00:09:34.253 --> 00:09:37.012
you can draw, and rate, the complete dependency

00:09:37.012 --> 00:09:38.736
map of any position, on sight.

00:09:40.035 --> 00:09:42.778
Pick one protocol you already use, and check

00:09:42.778 --> 00:09:45.395
it directly on a block explorer today.

00:09:45.395 --> 00:09:49.395
Next up, Lesson 5.1: bridges and trust assumptions.
